Chapter: FactArchive
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Foreword chapter Parse
  cites Foreword chapter Sha256
  cites Foreword chapter FactStore
  cites Foreword chapter ImportGate
  cites Foreword chapter FactLog
  cites Compress chapter Lz4
  cites Kernel chapter DiskFacts
  cites Kernel chapter AppPersist
  cites Works chapter KeyManager
  cites Works chapter RepoProtocolPersist

 Ancient history is tiered, not forgotten.

 The live log grows forever, and everything in it is equally present: the
 edition of a file written this morning and the edition it superseded a decade
 ago cost the same sector. There has never been a way to move the old one
 anywhere. Compaction cannot: it collapses two entries only when they are the
 same kind and the same bytes, because a compactor that cannot prove two facts
 are the same must keep both.

 So the base. The active repository records the signature of one compressed
 artifact and calls it the base, and from that moment the live log is not the
 history -- it is the delta on top of a base. A git packfile is this. An LSM
 tree's lower levels are this.

 The distinction this chapter exists to hold is that archiving is NOT deleting.
 An archived fact is still addressable by its hash, still verifies against it,
 and is still retrievable; the live log simply stops carrying the bytes and
 starts carrying a signed record of where they went. A store that has archived
 can still NAME everything it ever held. Only a store that forgets answers "not
 found". That is what keeps the founding promise -- a published definition
 exists forever -- while still reclaiming the sectors.

 What is archived is a RANGE OF THE LOG'S OWN SECTORS, copied out verbatim. So
 the artifact decompresses to a fact log: not a format resembling one, a copy of
 one, byte for byte, which the existing reader walks unchanged. A reader in 2626
 needs one log format and one decompressor, and both are in this tree.

 Where the artifact lives afterwards is a deployment concern and none of this
 chapter's business. It names it, signs it, and verifies it when handed it back.
 Whether the bytes sit on a second disk, an object store, a tape, or a peer
 answering a by-hash fetch is somebody else's decision, and encoding one here
 would be this chapter exceeding its authority.

 We say:

Section: Kind And Codec

 Kind 41, beside 39 (a path retired) and 40 (an index snapshot). The codec is
 named in the record rather than assumed by the reader, which is what makes
 changing it later a new base instead of a migration.

 Lz4 is the codec because when this shipped it was one of the few chapters in the
 compression quire that actually compressed: Deflate emitted stored blocks and
 Gzip wrapped that Deflate, so both GREW the artifact. Those two chapters have
 since been deleted rather than fixed, so Lz4 is now the only codec there is.
 A working gzip would probably be the better base if one existed -- not because
 of interop, which gzip always had even when it compressed nothing, but because
 it is the format every decoder on earth already reads. Moving is a new base,
 not a migration, which is the point of naming the codec in the record.

  persist-kind-base : Integer = 41

  fa-codec-lz4 : Text = "lz4"

 A base with no predecessor names one anyway. An empty field in a pipe-delimited
 line is a decoder's problem, and this line has enough fields already.

  fa-no-prev : Text = "-"

Section: The Base Record

 Both hashes are signed, and that is not belt-and-braces. The artifact hash is
 what you check on the bytes you were handed BEFORE you decompress them, which
 is the only order that can refuse a decompression bomb -- verify first, expand
 second. The plain hash is what you check on what came out, which catches a
 decoder that is subtly wrong rather than absent. A codec bug that silently
 produced different bytes would otherwise be indistinguishable from history.

  BaseRecord = record {
    br-artifact-hash : Text,
    br-plain-hash : Text,
    br-codec : Text,
    br-count : Integer,
    br-covers-to : Integer,
    br-prev-base : Text,
    br-signer : Text,
    br-signature : List Integer,
    br-author : Text,
    br-timestamp : Integer
  }

  ArchiveArtifact = record {
    aa-bytes : List Integer,
    aa-base : BaseRecord
  }

 The base of a store that has never archived. It is not a base and it does not
 verify -- no signer, no signature -- which is what an archival that found
 nothing to move should hand back.

  base-empty : BaseRecord
  base-empty = BaseRecord {
    br-artifact-hash = "", br-plain-hash = "", br-codec = fa-codec-lz4,
    br-count = 0, br-covers-to = 0, br-prev-base = fa-no-prev,
    br-signer = "", br-signature = [], br-author = "", br-timestamp = 0
  }

 The signature binds everything the record asserts except itself: both hashes,
 the codec, the count, what it covers, what it supersedes, and the attribution.
 An assertion is signed the way a tombstone is signed, and for the same reason
 -- an unauthenticated assertion is exactly the hole the store exists to close.

  base-signing-head : BaseRecord -> Text
  base-signing-head (b) =
    b.br-artifact-hash & "|" & b.br-plain-hash & "|" & b.br-codec

  base-signing-tail : BaseRecord -> Text
  base-signing-tail (b) =
    show (b.br-count) & "|" & show (b.br-covers-to) & "|" & b.br-prev-base
    & "|" & b.br-author & "|" & show (b.br-timestamp)

  base-signing-content : BaseRecord -> Text
  base-signing-content (b) = base-signing-head b & "|" & base-signing-tail b

Section: Wire Form

 Ten pipe-delimited fields. No field can contain a pipe: the hashes and the
 signature are hex, the codec is a bare word, the counts are decimal, and the
 fingerprints are colon-separated hex.

  base-to-text : BaseRecord -> Text
  base-to-text (b) =
    base-signing-head b & "|" & show (b.br-count) & "|" & show (b.br-covers-to)
    & "|" & b.br-prev-base & "|" & b.br-signer & "|" & bytes-to-hex (b.br-signature)
    & "|" & b.br-author & "|" & show (b.br-timestamp)

  base-from-text : Text -> Maybe BaseRecord
  base-from-text (line) =
    let parts = text-split line "|"
    in if list-length parts >= 10
      then Just (BaseRecord {
        br-artifact-hash = list-at parts 0,
        br-plain-hash = list-at parts 1,
        br-codec = list-at parts 2,
        br-count = parse-decimal-full (list-at parts 3),
        br-covers-to = parse-decimal-full (list-at parts 4),
        br-prev-base = list-at parts 5,
        br-signer = list-at parts 6,
        br-signature = hex-decode (list-at parts 7),
        br-author = list-at parts 8,
        br-timestamp = parse-decimal-full (list-at parts 9)
      })
      else None

Section: Reading The Log's Own Bytes

 A range of the live log, copied out as it sits. This is the whole reason the
 artifact is a fact log rather than something shaped like one.

 The byte-at-a-time accumulation is honest about its cost: it is linear in the
 range and allocates a cons per byte. An archival is a tool a human runs, not a
 path anything walks, so this is the cheap kind of expensive.

  fa-bytes-of : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  fa-bytes-of (buf) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else fa-bytes-of buf (i + 1) len (list-push acc (peek-byte buf i))

  fa-read-range : Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  fa-read-range (from-sector) (to-sector) =
    let count = to-sector - from-sector
    in if count <= 0 then []
    else let nbytes = count * fl-sector-size
    in let buf = alloc-bytes nbytes
    in let loaded = app-load-sectors from-sector count buf 0
    in fa-bytes-of buf 0 nbytes []

  fa-hash-bytes : List Integer -> Text
  fa-hash-bytes (bs) = sha256-to-hex (sha256 bs)

Section: Building A Base

 Compress the range, hash both forms, sign the assertion. The caller chooses the
 cut and says what the base supersedes; nothing here decides that, because
 choosing a cut requires knowing what is still current and that is the index's
 question, not this chapter's.

  archive-build : Integer, Integer, Integer, Text, Keypair, Integer -> ArchiveArtifact
  archive-build (from-sector) (to-sector) (count) (prev-base) (kp) (timestamp) =
    let plain = fa-read-range from-sector to-sector
    in let comp = lz4-compress plain
    in let unsigned = BaseRecord {
      br-artifact-hash = fa-hash-bytes comp,
      br-plain-hash = fa-hash-bytes plain,
      br-codec = fa-codec-lz4,
      br-count = count,
      br-covers-to = to-sector,
      br-prev-base = prev-base,
      br-signer = kp.fingerprint,
      br-signature = [],
      br-author = kp.fingerprint,
      br-timestamp = timestamp
    }
    in ArchiveArtifact {
      aa-bytes = comp,
      aa-base = base-signed unsigned kp
    }

  base-signed : BaseRecord, Keypair -> BaseRecord
  base-signed (b) (kp) =
    BaseRecord {
      br-artifact-hash = b.br-artifact-hash,
      br-plain-hash = b.br-plain-hash,
      br-codec = b.br-codec,
      br-count = b.br-count,
      br-covers-to = b.br-covers-to,
      br-prev-base = b.br-prev-base,
      br-signer = kp.fingerprint,
      br-signature = sign-content kp (base-signing-content b),
      br-author = b.br-author,
      br-timestamp = b.br-timestamp
    }

Section: Verifying A Base

 The same shape a tombstone verifies by: the signer must be a key the manifest
 holds at or above the threshold, and the signature must cover what the record
 claims.

  base-verify : BaseRecord, TrustManifest, Integer -> Boolean
  base-verify (b) (manifest) (threshold) =
    when manifest-lookup manifest (b.br-signer)
      is None -> False
      is Just (tk) -> if tk.score >= threshold
        then verify-content-signature (tk.public-key) (base-signing-content b) (b.br-signature)
        else False

Section: Opening An Archive

 The order of the three checks is the design. Verify the record first, so an
 untrusted base is refused before its bytes are touched at all. Verify the
 compressed bytes second, BEFORE expanding them, so a bomb is refused while it
 is still small. Only then decompress, and check what came out against the plain
 hash the same signature covered.

 A refusal is None. There is deliberately no variant saying which check failed:
 a caller holding an archive it cannot open has one recourse either way, and a
 differentiated refusal tells whoever handed it over which lie to fix.

  archive-open : List Integer, BaseRecord, TrustManifest, Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  archive-open (bytes) (base) (manifest) (threshold) =
    if base-verify base manifest threshold
      then archive-open-verified bytes base
      else None

  archive-open-verified : List Integer, BaseRecord -> Maybe (List Integer)
  archive-open-verified (bytes) (base) =
    if fa-hash-bytes bytes == base.br-artifact-hash
      then archive-expand bytes base
      else None

  archive-expand : List Integer, BaseRecord -> Maybe (List Integer)
  archive-expand (bytes) (base) =
    let plain = lz4-decompress bytes
    in if fa-hash-bytes plain == base.br-plain-hash then Just plain else None

Section: Which Facts May Leave

 Age is not the question, and assuming it was would destroy a repository. A file
 written once in 2026 and never touched since is ancient AND current; the tree
 still points at it. The question is whether a fact is the one currently
 DECIDING something, and only the index can answer that.

 So the rule, per fact:

   A definition may leave when it is not the current edition of its path. If
   wi-current points at its hash, it is the tree and it stays. If the path is
   not in the tree at all -- retired by a tombstone -- then no edition of it is
   current and every edition may leave.

   A tombstone may leave when its path IS in the tree, which means a later
   definition re-added the path and this tombstone lost. If the path is absent,
   this tombstone may be the one deciding that absence, so it stays. Several
   tombstones for one absent path are all kept: telling which one decides costs
   more than keeping them, and keeping is the safe direction.

   Everything else stays. A base record and an index snapshot are the lineage
   and the checkpoint. And every OTHER kind in the log -- 20 is browser history,
   24 is secret entries, 25 is FileShare manifests -- has no notion of currency
   that this chapter is entitled to invent. A compactor that cannot prove a fact
   is redundant must keep it, and so must an archiver.

 What this buys is the invariant: archive, rebuild, and the TREE is unchanged.
 Not the history -- wi-works loses the archived editions, which is the whole
 point, and they are in the base, still addressable and still verifying.

  def-archivable : WorkIndex, Text -> Boolean
  def-archivable (ix) (txt) =
    when source-def-from-text txt
      is None -> False
      is Just (d) -> when hamt-get (ix.wi-current) (d.sd-path)
        is None -> True
        is Just (h) -> h /= d.sd-hash

  tomb-archivable : WorkIndex, Text -> Boolean
  tomb-archivable (ix) (txt) =
    when hamt-get (ix.wi-current) (tombstone-path txt)
      is None -> False
      is Just (h) -> True

  entry-archivable : WorkIndex, LogEntry -> Boolean
  entry-archivable (ix) (e) =
    if e.le-kind == persist-kind-definition then def-archivable ix (e.le-text)
    else if e.le-kind == persist-kind-tombstone then tomb-archivable ix (e.le-text)
    else False

Section: Walking The Log

 Sector, size, kind and content per entry. The layout is FactLog's, asked for
 rather than known.

  LogEntry = record {
    le-sector : Integer,
    le-nsec : Integer,
    le-kind : Integer,
    le-text : Text
  }

 The sector buffer is allocated once and threaded through, not allocated per
 sector. There is no GC here: a fresh buffer per iteration would leave one
 512-byte block per sector of the log alive until the walk returned.

 The reads go through AppPersist's helpers rather than the block builtins.
 AppPersist grounds Device.Block and does not publish it, so this chapter stays
 effect-free like the rest of the repository readers. Reaching for
 block-read-sector directly is what CDX2031 stops, and correctly: this chapter
 has no business declaring itself a source of hardware effects.

  fa-scan-log : Integer, Integer, Integer, List LogEntry -> List LogEntry
  fa-scan-log (buf) (sector) (end-sec) (acc) =
    if sector >= end-sec then acc
    else let loaded = app-load-sectors sector 1 buf 0
    in let clen = fl-entry-content-len buf
    in if clen == 0 then fa-scan-log buf (sector + 1) end-sec acc
    else let nsec = fl-sectors-for clen
    in let k = fl-entry-kind buf
    in let txt = app-read-content sector clen
    in let entry = LogEntry { le-sector = sector, le-nsec = nsec, le-kind = k, le-text = txt }
    in fa-scan-log buf (sector + nsec) end-sec (list-push acc entry)

  fa-scan-from : Integer, Integer -> List LogEntry
  fa-scan-from (start) (end-sec) = fa-scan-log (alloc-bytes fl-sector-size) start end-sec []

Section: Choosing The Cut

 The cut is a PREFIX boundary, not a filter. A base is a contiguous range of the
 log copied out verbatim, so the archivable facts have to be at the bottom: the
 cut is the sector of the first fact that must stay, and everything below it
 goes. One fact that must stay near the start of the log stops the whole
 archival, and that is correct rather than unfortunate -- it is the difference
 between moving a range and rewriting history.

  archive-cut : List LogEntry, WorkIndex, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  archive-cut (entries) (ix) (i) (len) (head) =
    if i >= len then head
    else let e = list-at entries i
    in if entry-archivable ix e then archive-cut entries ix (i + 1) len head
    else e.le-sector

  count-below : List LogEntry, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  count-below (entries) (cut) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else if (list-at entries i).le-sector < cut then count-below entries cut (i + 1) len (acc + 1)
    else count-below entries cut (i + 1) len acc

Section: Running An Archival

 Copy the prefix out, shift what is left down over it, and record the base. The
 order matters: the artifact is built from the sectors BEFORE they are
 overwritten, and the base record is appended after the shift so it sits at the
 new head rather than in the range it describes.

  ArchiveRun = record {
    run-store : DiskFactStore,
    run-artifact : List Integer,
    run-base : BaseRecord,
    run-archived : Integer,
    run-cut : Integer
  }

 One sector at a time, low to high, through one reused buffer. Low-to-high is
 the safe direction here because the destination is always BELOW the source: an
 earlier write can never clobber a sector this loop has yet to read.

  fa-shift : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  fa-shift (buf) (src) (end-sec) (dst) (i) =
    if src + i >= end-sec then 0
    else let loaded = app-load-sectors (src + i) 1 buf 0
    in let w = write-fact-sectors buf (dst + i) 1 0
    in fa-shift buf src end-sec dst (i + 1)

 A store with nothing archivable is returned untouched, and says so with a cut
 equal to the log start and an empty artifact. That is the common case: a young
 log is all current.

  archive-run : DiskFactStore, TrustManifest, Integer, Keypair, Text, Integer -> ArchiveRun
  archive-run (store) (manifest) (threshold) (kp) (prev-base) (timestamp) =
    let sb = store.superblock
    in let head = sb.log-head
    in let ix = repo-index-verified store manifest threshold
    in let entries = fa-scan-from fact-log-start head
    in let cut = archive-cut entries ix 0 (list-length entries) head
    in if cut <= fact-log-start
      then ArchiveRun {
        run-store = store, run-artifact = [],
        run-base = base-empty, run-archived = 0, run-cut = fact-log-start
      }
      else archive-commit store entries cut head kp prev-base timestamp

  archive-commit : DiskFactStore, List LogEntry, Integer, Integer, Keypair, Text, Integer -> ArchiveRun
  archive-commit (store) (entries) (cut) (head) (kp) (prev-base) (timestamp) =
    let archived = count-below entries cut 0 (list-length entries) 0
    in let art = archive-build fact-log-start cut archived prev-base kp timestamp
    in let moved = fa-shift (alloc-bytes fl-sector-size) cut head fact-log-start 0
    in let sb = store.superblock
    in let shifted-head = fact-log-start + (head - cut)
    in let mid = DiskFactStore {
      superblock = Superblock {
        log-head = shifted-head,
        fact-count = (sb.fact-count) - archived,
        index-gen = (sb.index-gen) + 1
      },
      mem = store.mem
    }
    in ArchiveRun {
      run-store = base-save mid (art.aa-base),
      run-artifact = art.aa-bytes,
      run-base = art.aa-base,
      run-archived = archived,
      run-cut = cut
    }

Section: Reading A Work That May Have Left

 The difference between a tier and an amnesia, in one function. A hash the live
 log does not hold is not therefore unknown: if a base exists, the work may be in
 it, and the store can still NAME the artifact that would answer. What it must
 never do is claim the work is gone.

 WorkInBase carries the artifact hash and not the work, because resolving it
 needs bytes this box may not be holding. That is deployment's problem, and
 fetching them by hash is 6.2's.

 The live half is asked of the INDEX, not of DiskFacts. disk-read-fact answers
 from the store's in-memory map, which app-write-fact never populates -- and
 disk-load keys that map by the hash of the stored LINE, not by the hash of the
 content the line describes. It is an index of records; wi-works is an index of
 works, and a work's hash is what a quotation names. Asking the wrong one
 answers None for everything and reports every work as archived, which is how
 this was caught.

 The middle answer is WorkAskBase and not WorkInBase, and the difference is the
 whole honesty of this function. A store holding a base CANNOT tell an archived
 work from one it never had: deciding that needs the artifact, which it may not
 be holding. So the answer it can give is "not in the live log; here is the base
 to ask" -- a direction, not a claim. Naming that variant WorkInBase would have
 it assert membership it cannot check, and a hash nobody ever stored would come
 back as archived.

 The consequence, stated rather than hidden: once a base exists, WorkUnknown is
 only reachable when there is no base at all. A store cannot say "never stored"
 without reading its own history. Fixing that means putting membership evidence
 in the base record -- a Bloom filter over the archived hashes would do it in a
 few hundred bytes, and `codex/foreword/core/BloomFilter.codex` already exists --
 and it is a real option, not done here because nobody has needed it.

  WorkLookup =
    | WorkHere (SourceDefinition)
    | WorkAskBase (Text)
    | WorkUnknown

  work-lookup : WorkIndex, DiskFactStore, TrustManifest, Integer, Text -> WorkLookup
  work-lookup (ix) (store) (manifest) (threshold) (hash) =
    when hamt-get (ix.wi-works) hash
      is Just (d) -> WorkHere d
      is None -> when base-current store manifest threshold
        is Just (b) -> WorkAskBase (b.br-artifact-hash)
        is None -> WorkUnknown

Section: Reading A Work Out Of The Base

 This is what makes the tier answer instead of only pointing. work-lookup can say
 "ask the base"; this is the asking. Without it, "an archived work is still
 retrievable" is a claim about bytes rather than a thing anybody can do.

 The plain bytes are a fact log, so they are walked with FactLog's own readers
 rather than a second set written for a byte list -- that would be a fresh copy
 of the layout, which is exactly what FactLog exists to prevent and what
 AppPersist had to be cured of. The readers want an address, so the bytes go into
 a buffer once and each entry is read at `buf + offset`.

 The cost is one buffer the size of the base. An archival is a tool a human runs
 and a base is read when a quotation misses, so neither is a hot path; a store
 that wanted this often would keep an index of the base instead of scanning it.

  fa-to-buf : List Integer -> Integer
  fa-to-buf (bs) = fa-poke (alloc-bytes (list-length bs)) bs 0 (list-length bs)

  fa-poke : Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  fa-poke (buf) (bs) (i) (len) =
    if i >= len then buf
    else let w = poke-byte buf i (list-at bs i)
    in fa-poke buf bs (i + 1) len

  base-work : List Integer, Text -> Maybe SourceDefinition
  base-work (plain) (hash) =
    base-work-scan (fa-to-buf plain) 0 (list-length plain) hash

  base-work-scan : Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Maybe SourceDefinition
  base-work-scan (buf) (off) (total) (hash) =
    if off >= total then None
    else let clen = fl-entry-content-len (buf + off)
    in if clen == 0 then base-work-scan buf (off + fl-sector-size) total hash
    else let step = (fl-sectors-for clen) * fl-sector-size
    in if fl-entry-kind (buf + off) /= persist-kind-definition
      then base-work-scan buf (off + step) total hash
      else base-work-match buf off total step clen hash

  base-work-match : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Maybe SourceDefinition
  base-work-match (buf) (off) (total) (step) (clen) (hash) =
    when source-def-from-text (fl-text (buf + off) fl-header-size clen)
      is None -> base-work-scan buf (off + step) total hash
      is Just (d) -> if d.sd-hash == hash then Just d
                     else base-work-scan buf (off + step) total hash

 The whole path, in one call: verify the base, refuse the bytes if they are not
 what it signed, expand them, and find the work. A caller holding an artifact and
 a hash needs nothing else.

  base-resolve : List Integer, BaseRecord, TrustManifest, Integer, Text -> Maybe SourceDefinition
  base-resolve (bytes) (base) (manifest) (threshold) (hash) =
    when archive-open bytes base manifest threshold
      is None -> None
      is Just (plain) -> base-work plain hash

Section: The Base In The Active Repository

 The record lives in the live log as a fact, which is what "record the signature
 in the active repository as the base" means. The base records are never
 archived themselves -- they are small, one per archival rather than one per
 fact, and they are the lineage. Keeping them is what lets a store that holds
 only the newest artifact still account for every base that ever existed.

  base-save : DiskFactStore, BaseRecord -> DiskFactStore
  base-save (store) (b) =
    app-write-and-checkpoint store persist-kind-base (base-to-text b) (b.br-timestamp)

 The current base is the newest verified kind-41 in log order. An unsigned or
 untrusted base record is not the base, however recently it was appended.

  base-current : DiskFactStore, TrustManifest, Integer -> Maybe BaseRecord
  base-current (store) (manifest) (threshold) =
    let lines = app-scan-all store persist-kind-base
    in base-newest lines 0 (list-length lines) manifest threshold None

  base-newest : List Text, Integer, Integer, TrustManifest, Integer, Maybe BaseRecord -> Maybe BaseRecord
  base-newest (lines) (i) (len) (manifest) (threshold) (best) =
    if i >= len then best
    else base-newest lines (i + 1) len manifest threshold (base-pick (list-at lines i) manifest threshold best)

  base-pick : Text, TrustManifest, Integer, Maybe BaseRecord -> Maybe BaseRecord
  base-pick (line) (manifest) (threshold) (best) =
    when base-from-text line
      is None -> best
      is Just (b) -> if base-verify b manifest threshold then Just b else best
